<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pocahontas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1596–<a href="/years/1617">1617</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Rebecca Rolfe, Matoaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pocahontas was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>, paramount chief of the Powhatan, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. Captured by the English in 1613, she converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, took the name Rebecca and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> in 1614, a match that brought a brief peace. She travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1616 and died at Gravesend in 1617.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pocahontas.jpg" alt="Pocahontas" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon van de Passe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pocahontas_by_Simon_van_de_Passe_(1616).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
